  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle._grounding_kernel. Its documented rule says: \"The grounding verdict as the harness gives it, with no relaxation and the value-in-span check on.\" I read \"no relaxation\" as applying to the value-in-span check as well, so the value must occur in the span with the same case. The two reply texts are, in full: Reply A: {\"value\": \"ABC\", \"span\": \"abc\", \"document\": \"abc\"} and Reply B: {\"value\": \"ABC\", \"span\": \"ABC\", \"document\": \"ABC\"}. For Reply A the rule requires VALUE_NOT_IN_SPAN, because the value \"ABC\" is not in the span \"abc\" when case is respected. For Reply B the rule requires GROUNDED, because the value \"ABC\" is in the span \"ABC\". The function, however, returns GROUNDED for both, because it compares the casefolded normalized value and span. What would make me wrong: if the harness's own value-in-span check is case-insensitive, so that \"no relaxation\" does not govern the value-in-span comparison, then the rule does not require these two replies to be told apart.",
